---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const liftCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'rho', label: 'air density', min: 0.4, max: 1.3, step: 0.05, value: 1.225, unit: 'kg/m³' },
    { name: 'v', label: 'airspeed', min: 30, max: 250, step: 5, value: 80, unit: 'm/s' },
    { name: 'cl', label: 'lift coefficient', min: 0.2, max: 1.6, step: 0.05, value: 0.8, unit: '' },
    { name: 'S', label: 'wing area', min: 10, max: 130, step: 2, value: 30, unit: 'm²' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'lift = ½ρV²·S·CL', expr: '0.5*rho*v^2*S*cl', unit: 'N' },
    { label: 'mass this supports', expr: '0.5*rho*v^2*S*cl/9.81', unit: 'kg' },
  ],
};

const liftStepsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · A wing turns the flow', note: 'An airfoil is shaped and angled so the oncoming air leaves deflected downward. By Newton\'s third law, the air pushes the wing up.' },
    { math: '2 · Pressure does the accounting', note: 'Flow accelerates over the curved upper surface; by Bernoulli\'s principle its pressure drops. The suction above contributes most of the lift.' },
    { math: '3 · Circulation, formally', note: 'Mathematically, lift equals air density × speed × circulation (the Kutta–Joukowski theorem) — the rotating flow the wing imposes on the air.' },
    { math: '4 · Angle of attack tunes it', note: 'Lift grows roughly linearly with angle of attack — until the critical angle, when the flow separates and the wing stalls.' },
    { math: '5 · L = ½ρV²S·CL', note: 'Everything compresses into one equation: dynamic pressure × wing area × lift coefficient. Cruise is solved by balancing it against weight.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/aerospace-engineering/foundational/aerodynamics-i-lift-and-airfoils">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Flight is a momentum trade: a wing throws air downward, and the air pushes back up. Aerodynamics
    makes that trade quantitative with one master equation — lift equals half the air density times
    the square of the speed, times wing area, times a coefficient that captures the wing's shape
    and angle. Nearly all of applied aerodynamics is managing that coefficient.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how a wing makes lift" widget="equationStep" config={liftStepsCfg} />

  <h2>The lift equation as a design space</h2>
  <p>
    Every term is a lever. Slow aircraft get big wings; fast ones get small ones. Thin air — high
    altitude, hot days — demands more speed or more coefficient. Set the lift equal to an airliner's
    weight at cruise and you have found the wing every manufacturer actually built.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="drag the terms — find the wing that lifts the aircraft" widget="sliderLab" config={liftCfg} />

  <h2>Stall: the coefficient has a ceiling</h2>
  <p>
    Increase the angle of attack and lift rises — until about fifteen degrees, where the boundary
    layer separates, the suction collapses and the wing stalls. Stall is not an engine failure; it
    is an aerodynamic one, and it is recoverable by lowering the nose. Wings are fitted with slats
    and flaps precisely to raise this ceiling for takeoff and landing.
  </p>

  <h2>Where aerodynamics goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Speed brings compressibility: near the speed of sound, air piles into shock waves and drag
    spikes, which is why transonic aircraft sweep their wings. And every lift comes with drag
    induced by the downward momentum given to the air — the wingtip vortices you see in humid air
    behind a landing airliner. Lift is never free; it is a loan against drag.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET aerospace program criteria; Anderson, <em>Fundamentals of Aerodynamics</em>; FAA <em>Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
